🛠️ Solution & Detailed Explanation
🔍 Step 1: Identify Response Differences

Start by sending a login request with invalid credentials.
Capture the request using Burp Suite proxy.
Observe the response message returned by the server.
Usually, it says "Invalid username or password."
Repeat the request multiple times with different usernames.
Carefully compare each response.
Even a small difference matters in this lab.
Look for spaces, punctuation, or formatting issues.
These differences indicate a valid username.
This is the foundation of username enumeration.

📡 Step 2: Launch Username Enumeration Attack

Send the login request to Burp Intruder.
Mark the username field as a payload position.
Load the candidate username wordlist.
Configure payload type as "Simple List".
Go to Grep - Extract settings.
Select the error message from response.
This helps in comparing responses automatically.
Start the attack to test all usernames.
Wait for results to be generated.
Analyze the extracted responses carefully.

✏️ Step 3: Detect the Valid Username

After the attack completes, review the results.
Sort responses based on extracted error message.
One response will look slightly different.
It may contain a trailing space or typo.
This small difference is intentional.
It reveals that the username is valid.
Make note of this identified username.
This step reduces brute-force complexity.
Now you only need to guess the password.
This makes the attack much more efficient.

🔓 Step 4: Perform Password Brute-force

Now fix the username to the valid one.
Mark the password field as payload position.
Load the password wordlist into Intruder.
Clear previous username payloads.
Start the brute-force attack.
Observe the server responses closely.
Most responses will return failure messages.
But one request will behave differently.
Look for HTTP status code changes.
This indicates successful authentication.

🚀 Step 5: Identify Successful Login

During the attack, find the request with status code 302.
This indicates a redirect after successful login.
Normal failed attempts return 200 status code.
This difference is critical for detection.
Note the password used in this request.
Now you have both valid credentials.
This confirms the brute-force attack worked.
Always verify using response behavior.
This is a common real-world technique.
Automation tools make this process faster.

🛡️ Step 6: Access Account & Mitigation

Use the discovered username and password to log in.
Access the user account dashboard.
This completes the lab successfully.
The vulnerability is now fully exploited.

To prevent this issue, applications must respond consistently.
Error messages should be identical for all failures.
Avoid revealing any authentication hints.
Implement rate limiting to stop brute-force attacks.
Use account lockout mechanisms.
Security testing should detect such flaws early.
